## Runtime Environment

Run all project tools and tests through Docker (`make verify`, `docker compose run --rm <service>`).
Do not rely on host-installed project dependencies.

## Quality Requirements

`make verify` is the gate: a change is done only when it passes. Never weaken a check to make it
pass; change the code, or propose options.

Every behaviour change comes with unit tests, and unit-test coverage stays at or above the limit
in `pyproject.toml`. When fixing a bug, first write a test that fails on the current code, then fix
it.

The copy-paste detector runs in error mode: detected duplication fails verification.

The declared dependencies must match what the code imports: a missing, unused, only transitively
installed, or development-only dependency used by the product fails verification. A dependency
that runs only as a program is listed with its reason beside the checker's configuration.

Type annotations stay shallow and short: verification rejects a deeply nested or long annotation.
Give such a shape a name instead — a dataclass when its parts mean different things, a `type`
alias when the shape itself is the concept — rather than raising the limit.

Verification limits both kinds of complexity: how many paths a function has (cyclomatic, in
`src`) and how hard it is to follow (cognitive, in `src` and `tests`). A function over either
limit is split rather than the limit raised; the limits live in `compose.yaml` and
`pyproject.toml`.

Installed dependencies with a known vulnerability fail verification. Upgrade the dependency; an
advisory that cannot be fixed yet is ignored only with its reason written beside the ignore.

The dead-code detector scans product code only, so code that nothing but the tests uses counts as
dead and fails verification. A name used only from outside Python, such as a template, is listed
in a whitelist file under the reason it is used; a hook a library calls by convention goes into
`[tool.vulture]`.

Commits that add secrets, personal paths, local databases or log files are blocked by the secret
scanner (`config/security-rules.toml`); enable the hook once with `make install-hooks`.

## Code Layout

Code is layered `bootstrap → presentation | adapters → application → domain`: inner layers never
import outer ones, and presentation and adapters never import each other. Import-linter contracts
in `pyproject.toml` enforce this; Semgrep rules in `config/semgrep/` enforce what imports cannot
express. Each rule is tested against fixtures in `tests/fixtures/semgrep/` that must and must not
be flagged.

- **Domain** is split by kind of object: `entities` (equal when their identity is equal),
  `value_objects` (frozen, no mutable collections) and `services` (pure, no state between calls).
- **Application** is split into feature slices. A slice holds its use cases and `ports.py`.
  Anything that touches the outside world, including the filesystem, is reached through a port
  declared in the slice. A new capability adds a slice rather than growing shared modules.
- Domain and application code is pure: it imports only its own code and an allow-list of pure
  standard modules, nothing that reaches the network, files, processes, the environment, the clock
  or randomness, and no third-party library. A module that is pure joins the allow-list in the
  Semgrep rule; anything else is an adapter's work reached through a port.
- **Adapters** implement the ports and are the only code that talks to the database, the network,
  the clock or the disk. **Presentation** holds the delivery channels, such as the command line.
- `src/{{ package_name }}/bootstrap/` is the single composition root: the only place where concrete
  objects are constructed and wired together, and it contains no logic.
- The wall clock is read only by `adapters/system/clock.py`; everything else receives the time, so
  tests can fix it. The environment is read only by `bootstrap/`, an adapter's `configuration.py`
  and `presentation/defaults.py`; everything else receives its settings.
- One set of facts has one class. When an object is made from another that already holds those
  facts, it holds that object whole instead of copying its fields one by one; copying is right
  only at a boundary that changes the format.
- Functions that return `dict[str, Any]` across a module boundary become typed dataclasses.

Tests mirror the source tree: a test lives in the directory that matches the module it covers,
and helpers shared across areas live in `tests/support`. An architecture test rejects a test
directory that has no source counterpart.

A package does not grow into a long flat list of modules: past the limit in
`config/package-size.toml` it is split into subpackages by concept.

## Boy Scout Rule

Leave every piece of code you touch cleaner than you found it. When a change passes through code
with a small, safe design problem — a duplicated rule, a misplaced responsibility, a misleading
name — fix it in the same change, behaviour-preserving and covered by tests. A larger
restructuring goes into its own change.

When a change does not fit the structure it meets, change the structure rather than work around
it. A special case, a second helper beside one that already exists, a copied block, or a rule
weakened to pass is a workaround, not a substitute for the refactoring it avoids.

## Git Workflow

- Start every change from a feature branch created from the current `{{ default_branch }}`.
- Merge the latest `origin/{{ default_branch }}` into the branch (`git merge`, never rebase) and
  re-run `make verify` before opening a pull request.
- Never commit or push directly to `{{ default_branch }}`.

